# Commands run by the launchd job com.redwood.demo (see com.redwood.demo.plist).
# The data volume of iOS kept between boots (var_store=1 ufs_write=1; src/var_store.c): what the
# store held when this boot began, how much room the volume has, the line every run before this one
# left in /private/var and the names SpringBoard had made by then with the seconds they were
# written; then this run's own line, and after two and a half minutes of iOS left to itself the
# same names again.
# Nothing here saves. The job com.redwood.varstore stays for the run and writes the volume into the
# flash when the kernel says the run is ending -- which is the point: a run that was never told to
# save keeps what it wrote all the same. Run it twice over, with ./xl reboot in between; the second
# run's output holds the first run's line and the first run's seconds.
/redwood/bin/var_store show
/redwood/bin/vol_ls fs /private/var
/redwood/bin/vol_ls cat /private/var/redwood-stamp 4096
/redwood/bin/vol_ls ls /private/var/mobile/Library/SpringBoard
/redwood/bin/var_store stamp /private/var/redwood-stamp
sleep 150
/redwood/bin/vol_ls ls /private/var/mobile/Library/SpringBoard
/redwood/bin/vol_ls ls /private/var/mobile/Library
/redwood/bin/vol_ls cat /private/var/redwood-stamp 4096
/redwood/bin/var_store show
# (what the saver then did, and how many writes went into darwin, is at the end of the run: the
#  kernel's "var_store: the run is ending" and the loader's "ufs: N writes into darwin ... read
#  back the same; K refused")
